<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boulogne-sur-Mer</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gesoriacum, Boulogne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boulogne-sur-Mer is a port town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. As Gesoriacum it was a major Roman port and the base of the Classis Britannica, the fleet that guarded the Channel and linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> built a lighthouse there. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of the counts of Boulogne, and it later became France's leading fishing port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer.jpg" alt="Boulogne-sur-Mer" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthieu Debailleul · CC BY-SA 1.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulogne_vue_generale_phare_beffroi_mer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
